{"choices":[{"message":{"content":"{\n  \"schema_version\": \"focused_research.research_routes.v2\",\n  \"decision_clues\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"D1\",\n      \"evidence_id\": \"E_1a57cc1a35727685e9f15f16fcce3632d8444f2ff9d9e4d7c2220202e67e8ee8\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"The proposed design consists of a cascaded dielectric multilayer architecture combining SiOF, Si3N4, and BaSnO3 to achieve high visible-light transmittance together with broadband NIR rejection.\",\n      \"structure_or_method\": \"Cascaded dielectric multilayer architecture for spectral separation.\",\n      \"physical_effect\": \"Broadband NIR rejection while preserving daylight transmission via photonic band gap engineering.\",\n      \"conditions_and_unknowns\": [\n        \"Source targets visible transmission/NIR rejection; task requires UV transmission/visible-NIR rejection.\",\n        \"Materials (SiOF, Si3N4, BaSnO3) differ from the execution inventory (MgF2, Al2O3, etc.).\",\n        \"No numerical data for 255-280 nm range provided.\"\n      ],\n      \"goal_ids\": [\"G1\", \"G2\", \"G3\"],\n      \"inference_bridge\": \"Hypothesize that a similar cascaded stack topology, adapted to UV-transparent materials (MgF2/Al2O3), can create a short-wave pass characteristic allowing UV while blocking longer wavelengths.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"D2\",\n      \"evidence_id\": \"E_b35a9bfe8d10634bb5047d7712e1442b1fc755211809a1c37bfe0ec5bb64142e\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"Reflective multilayer coatings based on two dielectrics have higher peak performance but lower out-ofband rejection than Al/MgF 2 transmittance filters. The main material combinations are (MgF 2 or AlF 3 )/(LaF 3 or GdF 3 ).\",\n      \"structure_or_method\": \"All-dielectric reflective multilayers using fluoride pairs.\",\n      \"physical_effect\": \"High peak reflectance/transmittance in UV; trade-off in out-of-band rejection width.\",\n      \"conditions_and_unknowns\": [\n        \"Source discusses reflective mirrors or Al-based filters; task requires all-dielectric transmission filters.\",\n        \"LaF3/GdF3 are not in the execution inventory; MgF2 is available.\",\n        \"Specific layer counts for broad stopbands not detailed.\"\n      ],\n      \"goal_ids\": [\"G1\", \"G2\", \"G3\"],\n      \"inference_bridge\": \"Transfer the principle of using low-absorption fluorides (MgF2) paired with a higher-index stable oxide (Al2O3/HfO2) to construct a bandpass filter, accepting that wider stopbands may require more layers or specific stacking sequences.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"D3\",\n      \"evidence_id\": \"E_16bddf45a6a032b0569ac175d3ca201705aa5768bf01656dc06ccb4bb1839a7b\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"By tuning the optical thickness such that UV wavelengths (300-400 nm) fall near destructive-interference condi-tions, the coating effectively suppresses UV transmission [7]. In contrast, longer visible wavelengths lie closer to constructive interference, which preserves high transmittance in the 400-700 nm range.\",\n      \"structure_or_method\": \"Optical thickness tuning for wavelength-selective interference.\",\n      \"physical_effect\": \"Destructive interference for suppression bands, constructive for passbands.\",\n      \"conditions_and_unknowns\": [\n        \"Source describes UV blocking/Visible passing; task is the inverse (UV passing/Visible blocking).\",\n        \"Wavelength ranges differ (source 300-400 nm vs task 255-280 nm pass).\"\n      ],\n      \"goal_ids\": [\"G1\", \"G2\", \"G3\"],\n      \"inference_bridge\": \"Reverse the design logic: tune optical thickness so that 255-280 nm experiences constructive interference (high transmission) while 300-1100 nm experiences destructive interference (low transmission).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"D4\",\n      \"evidence_id\": \"E_d4eb6255a8e3d03f0e4fee21ef7752003839e9870034697490b67ddb2c2cfd73\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"The practical bandpass filter is composed of periodic SiO2/Si bilayers alternatively deposited layer-by-layer on the backside of a thermal oxidized Si substrate... The filter is composed of 32 layers, providing a total thickness of 6.02 m.\",\n      \"structure_or_method\": \"Periodic bilayer stacks for bandpass filtering.\",\n      \"physical_effect\": \"Interference effect creating a passband with wide stopband coverage.\",\n      \"conditions_and_unknowns\": [\n        \"Source uses Si/SiO2 (absorbing high-index) which is unsuitable for UV transmission.\",\n        \"Layer count (32) exceeds task limit (30).\",\n        \"Target wavelength (1550 nm) differs significantly from UV.\"\n      ],\n      \"goal_ids\": [\"G2\", \"G3\"],\n      \"inference_bridge\": \"Adapt the periodic bilayer concept using transparent UV materials (MgF2/Nb2O5) and optimize layer count to fit within 30 layers while targeting the UV passband.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"D5\",\n      \"evidence_id\": \"E_a85cac2616592ec900363fecab20c1f508ae66ce5afe9cb2244d5e7e218ade7e\",\n      \"source_quote\": \"To achieve an arbitrary BW, the refractive indices of the coating materials must be changed... assume that the high-index material is Nb 2 O 5 with n H = 2.25... and the low-index material is SiO 2 with n L = 1.45\",\n      \"structure_or_method\": \"Refractive index contrast manipulation for bandwidth control.\",\n      \"physical_effect\": \"Higher index contrast enables broader stopbands or narrower passbands depending on design.\",\n      \"conditions_and_unknowns\": [\n        \"Source targets 1550 nm; UV indices and dispersion will differ.\",\n        \"SiO2 absorption edge may be relevant below 200nm, but likely acceptable at 255nm.\",\n        \"Nb2O5 is in the execution inventory.\"\n      ],\n      \"goal_ids\": [\"G1\", \"G2\", \"G3\"],\n      \"inference_bridge\": \"Utilize the high index contrast between Nb2O5 (or HfO2/Ta2O5) and MgF2/SiO2 to maximize the stopband width covering 300-1100 nm with fewer layers.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"routes\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"R1\",\n      \"claim\": \"A symmetric Fabry-Perot cavity using MgF2 spacers and Nb2O5 mirrors can isolate the 255-280 nm band, leveraging high index contrast to widen the rejection bands towards visible and NIR.\",\n      \"clue_ids\": [\"D2\", \"D5\"],\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E_b35a9bfe8d10634bb5047d7712e1442b1fc755211809a1c37bfe0ec5bb64142e\", \"E_a85cac2616592ec900363fecab20c1f508ae66ce5afe9cb2244d5e7e218ade7e\"],\n      \"goal_ids\": [\"G1\", \"G2\", \"G3\"],\n      \"transfer_assumptions\": [\n        \"Nb2O5 and MgF2 maintain sufficient transparency and index contrast at 255 nm despite source data being at visible/NIR wavelengths.\",\n        \"A single-cavity design can provide adequate rejection across the broad 300-1100 nm range without exceeding 30 layers.\"\n      ],\n      \"compatibility_conditions\": [\n        {\"goal_id\": \"G1\", \"field\": \"wavelength_nm\", \"required_value\": [255, 280], \"reason\": \"Passband definition.\"},\n        {\"goal_id\": \"G2\", \"field\": \"wavelength_nm\", \"required_value\": [300, 700], \"reason\": \"Rejection band definition.\"},\n        {\"goal_id\": \"G3\", \"field\": \"wavelength_nm\", \"required_value\": [700, 1100], \"reason\": \"Rejection band definition.\"}\n      ],\n      \"family\": {\n        \"kind\": \"explicit_stack\",\n        \"incident_medium\": \"air\",\n        \"substrate\": \"fused_silica\",\n        \"layers\": [\n          {\"material\": \"Nb2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH1\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL1\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Nb2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH1\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL1\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Nb2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH1\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dCav\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Nb2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH1\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL1\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Nb2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH1\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL1\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Nb2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH1\"}\n        ],\n        \"thickness_variables_nm\": {\n          \"dH1\": [40, 60],\n          \"dL1\": [60, 90],\n          \"dCav\": [100, 150]\n        }\n      },\n      \"instances\": [\n        {\"id\": \"baseline\", \"parameters_nm\": {\"dH1\": 50, \"dL1\": 75, \"dCav\": 125}},\n        {\"id\": \"wider_stopband\", \"parameters_nm\": {\"dH1\": 45, \"dL1\": 80, \"dCav\": 125}}\n      ],\n      \"expectations\": [\n        {\n          \"goal_id\": \"G1\",\n          \"metric\": {\"objective_id\": \"G1\", \"metric\": \"mean_transmittance\", \"sense\": \"maximize\", \"mean_reduction\": \"wavelength_integral_mean\", \"region\": {\"wavelength_nm\": [255, 280], \"angle_deg\": 0, \"polarization\": \"unpolarized\"}},\n          \"baseline_instance_id\": \"baseline\",\n          \"candidate_instance_id\": \"wider_stopband\",\n          \"controlled_conditions\": \"Same stack symmetry, materials, and substrate.\",\n          \"changed_variables\": [\"dH1\", \"dL1\"],\n          \"direction\": \"unchanged\",\n          \"reason\": \"Baseline is tuned for peak; candidate adjusts mirror thicknesses primarily to affect stopband width, potentially slightly shifting but maintaining high UV transmission.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"goal_id\": \"G2\",\n          \"metric\": {\"objective_id\": \"G2\", \"metric\": \"mean_transmittance\", \"sense\": \"minimize\", \"mean_reduction\": \"wavelength_integral_mean\", \"region\": {\"wavelength_nm\": [300, 700], \"angle_deg\": 0, \"polarization\": \"unpolarized\"}},\n          \"baseline_instance_id\": \"baseline\",\n          \"candidate_instance_id\": \"wider_stopband\",\n          \"controlled_conditions\": \"Same stack symmetry, materials, and substrate.\",\n          \"changed_variables\": [\"dH1\", \"dL1\"],\n          \"direction\": \"decrease\",\n          \"reason\": \"Increasing index contrast effect via optimized quarter-wave matching in mirrors should deepen/widen the rejection band.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"open_requirements\": [],\n      \"readiness\": \"ready\",\n      \"basis_mode\": \"hybrid\",\n      \"model_knowledge_rationale\": \"Standard thin-film theory suggests that increasing the number of periods in the Bragg mirrors surrounding the cavity deepens the stopband. The specific thickness values are estimated based on quarter-wave optical thickness at the center of the UV passband (~267 nm), adjusted for the available material indices.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"R2\",\n      \"claim\": \"A multi-cavity design using Ta2O5 and SiO2 can sharpen the passband edges, improving rejection in the adjacent visible band (300-400 nm) while maintaining UV transmission.\",\n      \"clue_ids\": [\"D4\"],\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E_d4eb6255a8e3d03f0e4fee21ef7752003839e9870034697490b67ddb2c2cfd73\"],\n      \"goal_ids\": [\"G1\", \"G2\"],\n      \"transfer_assumptions\": [\n        \"Ta2O5 is sufficiently transparent at 255 nm (some absorption may occur near 250 nm, requiring verification).\",\n        \"Multi-cavity coupling can be achieved within the 30-layer limit.\"\n      ],\n      \"compatibility_conditions\": [\n        {\"goal_id\": \"G1\", \"field\": \"wavelength_nm\", \"required_value\": [255, 280], \"reason\": \"Passband definition.\"}\n      ],\n      \"family\": {\n        \"kind\": \"explicit_stack\",\n        \"incident_medium\": \"air\",\n        \"substrate\": \"fused_silica\",\n        \"layers\": [\n          {\"material\": \"Ta2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"},\n          {\"material\": \"SiO2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Ta2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"},\n          {\"material\": \"SiO2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dS\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Ta2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"},\n          {\"material\": \"SiO2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Ta2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"},\n          {\"material\": \"SiO2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dS\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Ta2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"},\n          {\"material\": \"SiO2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Ta2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"}\n        ],\n        \"thickness_variables_nm\": {\n          \"dH\": [35, 55],\n          \"dL\": [55, 85],\n          \"dS\": [100, 140]\n        }\n      },\n      \"instances\": [\n        {\"id\": \"single_cavity_ref\", \"parameters_nm\": {\"dH\": 45, \"dL\": 70, \"dS\": 120}},\n        {\"id\": \"coupled_cavity\", \"parameters_nm\": {\"dH\": 45, \"dL\": 70, \"dS\": 110}}\n      ],\n      \"expectations\": [\n        {\n          \"goal_id\": \"G1\",\n          \"metric\": {\"objective_id\": \"G1\", \"metric\": \"mean_transmittance\", \"sense\": \"maximize\", \"mean_reduction\": \"wavelength_integral_mean\", \"region\": {\"wavelength_nm\": [255, 280], \"angle_deg\": 0, \"polarization\": \"unpolarized\"}},\n          \"baseline_instance_id\": \"single_cavity_ref\",\n          \"candidate_instance_id\": \"coupled_cavity\",\n          \"controlled_conditions\": \"Same material order and layer count.\",\n          \"changed_variables\": [\"dS\"],\n          \"direction\": \"increase\",\n          \"reason\": \"Coupling cavities can flatten the passband top, potentially increasing the integrated mean transmission over the 25 nm band.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"goal_id\": \"G2\",\n          \"metric\": {\"objective_id\": \"G2\", \"metric\": \"mean_transmittance\", \"sense\": \"minimize\", \"mean_reduction\": \"wavelength_integral_mean\", \"region\": {\"wavelength_nm\": [300, 700], \"angle_deg\": 0, \"polarization\": \"unpolarized\"}},\n          \"baseline_instance_id\": \"single_cavity_ref\",\n          \"candidate_instance_id\": \"coupled_cavity\",\n          \"controlled_conditions\": \"Same material order and layer count.\",\n          \"changed_variables\": [\"dS\"],\n          \"direction\": \"decrease\",\n          \"reason\": \"Multi-cavity designs typically exhibit steeper skirts, reducing transmission in the immediate adjacent visible band.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"open_requirements\": [\"Verify Ta2O5 absorption coefficient at 255 nm in the local dataset before execution.\"],\n      \"readiness\": \"needs_revision\",\n      \"basis_mode\": \"external_evidence\",\n      \"model_knowledge_rationale\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"R3\",\n      \"claim\": \"Using HfO2 as the high-index material instead of Nb2O5 may offer better UV transparency margin, allowing a thicker stack for deeper NIR rejection without excessive absorption loss in the passband.\",\n      \"clue_ids\": [\"D5\"],\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E_a85cac2616592ec900363fecab20c1f508ae66ce5afe9cb2244d5e7e218ade7e\"],\n      \"goal_ids\": [\"G1\", \"G3\"],\n      \"transfer_assumptions\": [\n        \"HfO2 has a wider bandgap than Nb2O5, resulting in lower absorption at 255 nm.\",\n        \"The index contrast of HfO2/MgF2 is sufficient to create effective stopbands.\"\n      ],\n      \"compatibility_conditions\": [],\n      \"family\": {\n        \"kind\": \"explicit_stack\",\n        \"incident_medium\": \"air\",\n        \"substrate\": \"fused_silica\",\n        \"layers\": [\n          {\"material\": \"HfO2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL\"},\n          {\"material\": \"HfO2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL\"},\n          {\"material\": \"HfO2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL\"},\n          {\"material\": \"HfO2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL\"},\n          {\"material\": \"HfO2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL\"},\n          {\"material\": \"HfO2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL\"},\n          {\"material\": \"HfO2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"}\n        ],\n        \"thickness_variables_nm\": {\n          \"dH\": [30, 50],\n          \"dL\": [50, 80]\n        }\n      },\n      \"instances\": [\n        {\"id\": \"low_layers\", \"parameters_nm\": {\"dH\": 40, \"dL\": 65}},\n        {\"id\": \"high_layers\", \"parameters_nm\": {\"dH\": 40, \"dL\": 65}}\n      ],\n      \"expectations\": [],\n      \"open_requirements\": [\"Define distinct thickness instances for comparison; current instances are identical placeholders.\", \"Confirm HfO2 refractive index data availability at 255 nm.\"],\n      \"readiness\": \"needs_revision\",\n      \"basis_mode\": \"model_knowledge\",\n      \"model_knowledge_rationale\": \"Hafnia (HfO2) is widely known in UV optics for its transparency down to ~200 nm, superior to Titania and often Niobia. A simple periodic stack (Bragg mirror) centered at the UV band edge could serve as a short-pass filter if designed to reflect longer wavelengths. However, a pure periodic stack reflects the center wavelength; to transmit UV and block VIS/NIR, the stack must be designed as a long-pass or specific bandpass. This route proposes testing a high-periodicity Bragg stack centered at ~350nm to reflect VIS/NIR while transmitting UV (if the stopband is positioned correctly).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"R4\",\n      \"claim\": \"A 'rugate-like' discrete approximation using varying thicknesses of Al2O3 and MgF2 can suppress side-lobes in the visible region, improving rejection uniformity.\",\n      \"clue_ids\": [\"D1\"],\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E_1a57cc1a35727685e9f15f16fcce3632d8444f2ff9d9e4d7c2220202e67e8ee8\"],\n      \"goal_ids\": [\"G2\", \"G3\"],\n      \"transfer_assumptions\": [\n        \"Discrete layer steps can approximate a graded index profile sufficiently to reduce sidelobes.\",\n        \"Al2O3 is transparent enough at 255 nm.\"\n      ],\n      \"compatibility_conditions\": [],\n      \"family\": {\n        \"kind\": \"explicit_stack\",\n        \"incident_medium\": \"air\",\n        \"substrate\": \"fused_silica\",\n        \"layers\": [\n          {\"material\": \"Al2O3\", \"thickness_nm\": \"d1\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"d2\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Al2O3\", \"thickness_nm\": \"d3\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"d4\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Al2O3\", \"thickness_nm\": \"d5\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"d6\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Al2O3\", \"thickness_nm\": \"d7\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"d8\"}\n        ],\n        \"thickness_variables_nm\": {\n          \"d1\": [20, 40], \"d2\": [40, 60], \"d3\": [30, 50], \"d4\": [50, 70],\n          \"d5\": [30, 50], \"d6\": [50, 70], \"d7\": [20, 40], \"d8\": [40, 60]\n        }\n      },\n      \"instances\": [\n        {\"id\": \"uniform_qw\", \"parameters_nm\": {\"d1\": 30, \"d2\": 50, \"d3\": 30, \"d4\": 50, \"d5\": 30, \"d6\": 50, \"d7\": 30, \"d8\": 50}},\n        {\"id\": \"apodized\", \"parameters_nm\": {\"d1\": 25, \"d2\": 45, \"d3\": 35, \"d4\": 55, \"d5\": 35, \"d6\": 55, \"d7\": 25, \"d8\": 45}}\n      ],\n      \"expectations\": [\n        {\n          \"goal_id\": \"G2\",\n          \"metric\": {\"objective_id\": \"G2\", \"metric\": \"mean_transmittance\", \"sense\": \"minimize\", \"mean_reduction\": \"wavelength_integral_mean\", \"region\": {\"wavelength_nm\": [300, 700], \"angle_deg\": 0, \"polarization\": \"unpolarized\"}},\n          \"baseline_instance_id\": \"uniform_qw\",\n          \"candidate_instance_id\": \"apodized\",\n          \"controlled_conditions\": \"Same layer count and materials.\",\n          \"changed_variables\": [\"d1\", \"d2\", \"d3\", \"d4\", \"d5\", \"d6\", \"d7\", \"d8\"],\n          \"direction\": \"decrease\",\n          \"reason\": \"Apodization (varying layer thicknesses) is theoretically expected to reduce sidelobe levels in the stopband compared to a uniform periodic stack.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"open_requirements\": [],\n      \"readiness\": \"ready\",\n      \"basis_mode\": \"hybrid\",\n      \"model_knowledge_rationale\": \"Rugate filters theoretically eliminate sidelobes by continuous index variation. In discrete multilayers, stepping the optical thicknesses (apodization) mimics this effect. Al2O3 is chosen for its robustness and moderate index.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"R5\",\n      \"claim\": \"Exploiting the dispersion of MgF2 and Nb2O5, a non-quarter-wave stack optimized specifically for 255-280 nm transmission can achieve broader NIR rejection than a standard quarter-wave design centered at the same wavelength.\",\n      \"clue_ids\": [\"D3\"],\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E_16bddf45a6a032b0569ac175d3ca201705aa5768bf01656dc06ccb4bb1839a7b\"],\n      \"goal_ids\": [\"G1\", \"G3\"],\n      \"transfer_assumptions\": [\n        \"Numerical optimization of non-standard thicknesses can leverage material dispersion to widen the stopband.\",\n        \"Dispersion data in the execution inventory is accurate for the UV range.\"\n      ],\n      \"compatibility_conditions\": [],\n      \"family\": {\n        \"kind\": \"explicit_stack\",\n        \"incident_medium\": \"air\",\n        \"substrate\": \"fused_silica\",\n        \"layers\": [\n          {\"material\": \"Nb2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"t1\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"t2\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Nb2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"t3\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"t4\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Nb2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"t5\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"t6\"}\n        ],\n        \"thickness_variables_nm\": {\n          \"t1\": [20, 80], \"t2\": [40, 120], \"t3\": [20, 80], \"t4\": [40, 120], \"t5\": [20, 80], \"t6\": [40, 120]\n        }\n      },\n      \"instances\": [\n        {\"id\": \"qw_design\", \"parameters_nm\": {\"t1\": 45, \"t2\": 75, \"t3\": 45, \"t4\": 75, \"t5\": 45, \"t6\": 75}},\n        {\"id\": \"optimized_non_qw\", \"parameters_nm\": {\"t1\": 35, \"t2\": 90, \"t3\": 55, \"t4\": 65, \"t5\": 40, \"t6\": 85}}\n      ],\n      \"expectations\": [\n        {\n          \"goal_id\": \"G3\",\n          \"metric\": {\"objective_id\": \"G3\", \"metric\": \"mean_transmittance\", \"sense\": \"minimize\", \"mean_reduction\": \"wavelength_integral_mean\", \"region\": {\"wavelength_nm\": [700, 1100], \"angle_deg\": 0, \"polarization\": \"unpolarized\"}},\n          \"baseline_instance_id\": \"qw_design\",\n          \"candidate_instance_id\": \"optimized_non_qw\",\n          \"controlled_conditions\": \"Same materials and layer count.\",\n          \"changed_variables\": [\"t1\", \"t2\", \"t3\", \"t4\", \"t5\", \"t6\"],\n          \"direction\": \"decrease\",\n          \"reason\": \"Non-quarter-wave thicknesses can shift higher-order harmonics or broaden the fundamental stopband through constructive interference of reflections at different wavelengths.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"open_requirements\": [],\n      \"readiness\": \"ready\",\n      \"basis_mode\": \"external_evidence\",\n      \"model_knowledge_rationale\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"R6\",\n      \"claim\": \"A hybrid stack starting with a MgF2/Al2O3 UV antireflection section followed by a Nb2O5/SiO2 high-reflector section can maximize UV entry while blocking VIS/NIR.\",\n      \"clue_ids\": [\"D1\", \"D2\"],\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E_1a57cc1a35727685e9f15f16fcce3632d8444f2ff9d9e4d7c2220202e67e8ee8\", \"E_b35a9bfe8d10634bb5047d7712e1442b1fc755211809a1c37bfe0ec5bb64142e\"],\n      \"goal_ids\": [\"G1\", \"G2\", \"G3\"],\n      \"transfer_assumptions\": [\n        \"Combining different material pairs in one stack is feasible without interlayer diffusion issues (assumed ideal interfaces).\",\n        \"SiO2 and Al2O3 have compatible stress properties.\"\n      ],\n      \"compatibility_conditions\": [],\n      \"family\": {\n        \"kind\": \"explicit_stack\",\n        \"incident_medium\": \"air\",\n        \"substrate\": \"fused_silica\",\n        \"layers\": [\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dAR\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Al2O3\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dAR2\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Nb2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"},\n          {\"material\": \"SiO2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Nb2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"},\n          {\"material\": \"SiO2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Nb2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"},\n          {\"material\": \"SiO2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL\"}\n        ],\n        \"thickness_variables_nm\": {\n          \"dAR\": [50, 100], \"dAR2\": [20, 50], \"dH\": [30, 60], \"dL\": [50, 90]\n        }\n      },\n      \"instances\": [\n        {\"id\": \"no_ar\", \"parameters_nm\": {\"dAR\": 0, \"dAR2\": 0, \"dH\": 45, \"dL\": 70}},\n        {\"id\": \"with_ar\", \"parameters_nm\": {\"dAR\": 70, \"dAR2\": 35, \"dH\": 45, \"dL\": 70}}\n      ],\n      \"expectations\": [\n        {\n          \"goal_id\": \"G1\",\n          \"metric\": {\"objective_id\": \"G1\", \"metric\": \"mean_transmittance\", \"sense\": \"maximize\", \"mean_reduction\": \"wavelength_integral_mean\", \"region\": {\"wavelength_nm\": [255, 280], \"angle_deg\": 0, \"polarization\": \"unpolarized\"}},\n          \"baseline_instance_id\": \"no_ar\",\n          \"candidate_instance_id\": \"with_ar\",\n          \"controlled_conditions\": \"Same reflector section thicknesses.\",\n          \"changed_variables\": [\"dAR\", \"dAR2\"],\n          \"direction\": \"increase\",\n          \"reason\": \"Adding an antireflection section matched to the UV band should reduce surface reflection losses, increasing net transmission.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"open_requirements\": [\"Execution system must support mixed material definitions in a single family.\"],\n      \"readiness\": \"needs_revision\",\n      \"basis_mode\": \"hybrid\",\n      \"model_knowledge_rationale\": \"Standard optical design practice often combines AR coatings with filter stacks. Using low-index MgF2 as the outermost layer reduces Fresnel reflection from air.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"R7\",\n      \"claim\": \"Increasing the total layer count towards the 30-layer limit using a symmetric Bragg stack centered at 350 nm can create a sharp cut-on edge that blocks 300-700 nm while transmitting <300 nm (including 255-280 nm).\",\n      \"clue_ids\": [\"D4\"],\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E_d4eb6255a8e3d03f0e4fee21ef7752003839e9870034697490b67ddb2c2cfd73\"],\n      \"goal_ids\": [\"G1\", \"G2\"],\n      \"transfer_assumptions\": [\n        \"A long-pass characteristic (transmitting UV, blocking VIS) can be achieved by placing the stopband in the visible.\",\n        \"Material absorption does not dominate in the UV for thick stacks.\"\n      ],\n      \"compatibility_conditions\": [],\n      \"family\": {\n        \"kind\": \"explicit_stack\",\n        \"incident_medium\": \"air\",\n        \"substrate\": \"fused_silica\",\n        \"layers\": [\n          {\"material\": \"Nb2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Nb2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Nb2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Nb2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Nb2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Nb2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Nb2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Nb2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"}\n        ],\n        \"thickness_variables_nm\": {\n          \"dH\": [40, 70],\n          \"dL\": [60, 100]\n        }\n      },\n      \"instances\": [\n        {\"id\": \"15_layer\", \"parameters_nm\": {\"dH\": 55, \"dL\": 80}},\n        {\"id\": \"29_layer\", \"parameters_nm\": {\"dH\": 55, \"dL\": 80}}\n      ],\n      \"expectations\": [],\n      \"open_requirements\": [\"Family definition currently lists 15 layers; need to expand to ~29 layers to test the 'limit' hypothesis properly.\", \"Define distinct thickness sets for the 29-layer instance if optimization is intended.\"],\n      \"readiness\": \"needs_revision\",\n      \"basis_mode\": \"external_evidence\",\n      \"model_knowledge_rationale\": \"More layers in a Bragg stack generally increase the reflectance (rejection) depth and width. Centering the stopband at 350 nm should block the 300-700 nm range (partially) and definitely the 700+ nm range, while leaving the 255-280 nm range in the transmission tail.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"R8\",\n      \"claim\": \"Using SiO2 instead of MgF2 as the low-index material with Nb2O5 may simplify deposition but risks higher absorption or reduced index contrast in the deep UV, potentially lowering UV transmission.\",\n      \"clue_ids\": [\"D5\"],\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E_a85cac2616592ec900363fecab20c1f508ae66ce5afe9cb2244d5e7e218ade7e\"],\n      \"goal_ids\": [\"G1\"],\n      \"transfer_assumptions\": [\n        \"SiO2 transmission at 255 nm is comparable to MgF2.\",\n        \"Index contrast reduction (SiO2 n~1.5 vs MgF2 n~1.38) is the primary variable.\"\n      ],\n      \"compatibility_conditions\": [],\n      \"family\": {\n        \"kind\": \"explicit_stack\",\n        \"incident_medium\": \"air\",\n        \"substrate\": \"fused_silica\",\n        \"layers\": [\n          {\"material\": \"Nb2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"},\n          {\"material\": \"SiO2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Nb2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"},\n          {\"material\": \"SiO2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Nb2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"},\n          {\"material\": \"SiO2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL\"}\n        ],\n        \"thickness_variables_nm\": {\n          \"dH\": [40, 60],\n          \"dL\": [50, 80]\n        }\n      },\n      \"instances\": [\n        {\"id\": \"mgf2_baseline\", \"parameters_nm\": {\"dH\": 50, \"dL\": 65}},\n        {\"id\": \"sio2_variant\", \"parameters_nm\": {\"dH\": 50, \"dL\": 65}}\n      ],\n      \"expectations\": [\n        {\n          \"goal_id\": \"G1\",\n          \"metric\": {\"objective_id\": \"G1\", \"metric\": \"mean_transmittance\", \"sense\": \"maximize\", \"mean_reduction\": \"wavelength_integral_mean\", \"region\": {\"wavelength_nm\": [255, 280], \"angle_deg\": 0, \"polarization\": \"unpolarized\"}},\n          \"baseline_instance_id\": \"mgf2_baseline\",\n          \"candidate_instance_id\": \"sio2_variant\",\n          \"controlled_conditions\": \"Same geometry, different low-index material (requires separate family definition in execution, here simulated by parameter swap assumption).\",\n          \"changed_variables\": [],\n          \"direction\": \"decrease\",\n          \"reason\": \"Lower index contrast with SiO2 may require more layers for same rejection, or result in higher reflection loss in the passband if not re-optimized. Also potential absorption differences.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"open_requirements\": [\"This comparison requires changing the material identity, which violates the 'thickness intervention within one family' constraint for a single route instance generation. This route serves as a hypothesis for material selection rather than a direct thickness tweak unless the execution engine supports material swapping via parameter mapping (unlikely).\"],\n      \"readiness\": \"needs_revision\",\n      \"basis_mode\": \"model_knowledge\",\n      \"model_knowledge_rationale\": \"MgF2 is generally preferred for deep UV due to lower refractive index and good transparency. SiO2 is a common alternative but may have higher absorption edges near 250 nm depending on deposition. This route highlights the material choice trade-off.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"R9\",\n      \"claim\": \"A detuned stack where layer thicknesses gradually increase from the substrate to the incident medium can compensate for field distribution asymmetry, potentially improving damage threshold or transmission uniformity.\",\n      \"clue_ids\": [],\n      \"evidence_ids\": [],\n      \"goal_ids\": [\"G1\", \"G2\"],\n      \"transfer_assumptions\": [\n        \"Field distribution in the UV passband is asymmetric due to substrate index mismatch.\",\n        \"Thickness grading can mitigate standing wave peaks.\"\n      ],\n      \"compatibility_conditions\": [],\n      \"family\": {\n        \"kind\": \"explicit_stack\",\n        \"incident_medium\": \"air\",\n        \"substrate\": \"fused_silica\",\n        \"layers\": [\n          {\"material\": \"HfO2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dSub\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL1\"},\n          {\"material\": \"HfO2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dMid\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL2\"},\n          {\"material\": \"HfO2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dSurf\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL3\"}\n        ],\n        \"thickness_variables_nm\": {\n          \"dSub\": [30, 50], \"dMid\": [35, 55], \"dSurf\": [40, 60],\n          \"dL1\": [50, 70], \"dL2\": [55, 75], \"dL3\": [60, 80]\n        }\n      },\n      \"instances\": [\n        {\"id\": \"uniform\", \"parameters_nm\": {\"dSub\": 40, \"dMid\": 40, \"dSurf\": 40, \"dL1\": 60, \"dL2\": 60, \"dL3\": 60}},\n        {\"id\": \"graded\", \"parameters_nm\": {\"dSub\": 35, \"dMid\": 40, \"dSurf\": 45, \"dL1\": 55, \"dL2\": 60, \"dL3\": 65}}\n      ],\n      \"expectations\": [\n        {\n          \"goal_id\": \"G1\",\n          \"metric\": {\"objective_id\": \"G1\", \"metric\": \"mean_transmittance\", \"sense\": \"maximize\", \"mean_reduction\": \"wavelength_integral_mean\", \"region\": {\"wavelength_nm\": [255, 280], \"angle_deg\": 0, \"polarization\": \"unpolarized\"}},\n          \"baseline_instance_id\": \"uniform\",\n          \"candidate_instance_id\": \"graded\",\n          \"controlled_conditions\": \"Same average optical thickness.\",\n          \"changed_variables\": [\"dSub\", \"dMid\", \"dSurf\", \"dL1\", \"dL2\", \"dL3\"],\n          \"direction\": \"increase\",\n          \"reason\": \"Grading may better match the impedance profile, reducing ripple in the passband.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"open_requirements\": [],\n      \"readiness\": \"ready\",\n      \"basis_mode\": \"model_knowledge\",\n      \"model_knowledge_rationale\": \"Impedance matching concepts from microwave engineering applied to optics suggest that graded index profiles reduce reflections. In discrete layers, stepping the thicknesses approximates this gradient.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"R10\",\n      \"claim\": \"Incorporating a half-wave absentee layer of MgF2 within a Nb2O5 stack can act as a coupling element to split the passband or widen it, useful for covering the full 255-280 nm range if a single peak is too narrow.\",\n      \"clue_ids\": [\"D4\"],\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E_d4eb6255a8e3d03f0e4fee21ef7752003839e9870034697490b67ddb2c2cfd73\"],\n      \"goal_ids\": [\"G1\"],\n      \"transfer_assumptions\": [\n        \"A half-wave layer at the center wavelength acts as an absentee layer, effectively coupling two cavities.\",\n        \"This coupling widens the passband.\"\n      ],\n      \"compatibility_conditions\": [],\n      \"family\": {\n        \"kind\": \"explicit_stack\",\n        \"incident_medium\": \"air\",\n        \"substrate\": \"fused_silica\",\n        \"layers\": [\n          {\"material\": \"Nb2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Nb2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dCav\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Nb2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"},\n          {\"material\": \"MgF2\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dL\"},\n          {\"material\": \"Nb2O5\", \"thickness_nm\": \"dH\"}\n        ],\n        \"thickness_variables_nm\": {\n          \"dH\": [40, 60],\n          \"dL\": [60, 90],\n          \"dCav\": [100, 200]\n        }\n      },\n      \"instances\": [\n        {\"id\": \"single_peak\", \"parameters_nm\": {\"dH\": 50, \"dL\": 75, \"dCav\": 130}},\n        {\"id\": \"wide_pass\", \"parameters_nm\": {\"dH\": 50, \"dL\": 75, \"dCav\": 260}}\n      ],\n      \"expectations\": [\n        {\n          \"goal_id\": \"G1\",\n          \"metric\": {\"objective_id\": \"G1\", \"metric\": \"mean_transmittance\", \"sense\": \"maximize\", \"mean_reduction\": \"wavelength_integral_mean\", \"region\": {\"wavelength_nm\": [255, 280], \"angle_deg\": 0, \"polarization\": \"unpolarized\"}},\n          \"baseline_instance_id\": \"single_peak\",\n          \"candidate_instance_id\": \"wide_pass\",\n          \"controlled_conditions\": \"Same mirror thicknesses.\",\n          \"changed_variables\": [\"dCav\"],\n          \"direction\": \"increase\",\n          \"reason\": \"Doubling the cavity thickness (half-wave instead of quarter-wave equivalent or similar coupling condition) can split the resonance into two peaks, widening the effective passband to cover the 25 nm requirement.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"open_requirements\": [],\n      \"readiness\": \"ready\",\n      \"basis_mode\": \"hybrid\",\n      \"model_knowledge_rationale\": \"Filter theory indicates that increasing the spacer thickness in a Fabry-Perot etalon changes the free spectral range. Properly choosing a thicker spacer can align multiple transmission peaks or broaden the single peak depending on the exact design, often used to widen narrow bandpass filters.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}","role":"assistant"},"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"logprobs":null}],"object":"chat.completion","usage":{"prompt_tokens":39386,"completion_tokens":11053,"total_tokens":50439,"completion_tokens_details":{"text_tokens":11053},"prompt_tokens_details":{"text_tokens":39386}},"created":1792117671,"system_fingerprint":null,"model":"qwen3.5-plus","id":"chatcmpl-a0dc6821-ecbb-92fa-a1d7-51ce84018a7c"}